    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Refine the fastest measured design: flat row-major matrix storage, 64-bit vertical column sums, and a rolling horizontal window. Its mean was 0.03473 s, versus 0.12991 s for baseline. Explore low-risk improvements to this serial kernel and input handling, measuring each change independently; retain 64-bit intermediates and correctness for every valid input.",
        "B": "Explore an alternative locality- or memory-focused O(N²) design, such as a carefully tuned streaming/ring-buffer or tiled variant. A prior ring-buffer candidate measured 0.05961 s, so treat reduced storage as a hypothesis rather than an assumed speedup. Compare against A on end-to-end time and preserve correct handling of arbitrary matrix values and all valid N,S.",
        "C": "Investigate whether parallelism or SIMD can help without adding more overhead than useful work. The measured OpenMP candidates (0.05761 and 0.05829 s) were slower than A on this four-core ARM64 target. Test conservative thresholds and balanced partitions, avoid excessive duplicated setup or memory traffic, and retain a serial path when it measures better. Validate each variant fully.",
        "G": "Use A's 0.03473 s candidate as the provisional integration base; the measured B and C alternatives were about 0.057–0.059 s. Validate the integrated program against the oracle and measure the complete result: do not assume SIMD, threading, or memory-saving changes combine additively. Keep only changes that independently preserve correctness for every valid input and improve measured end-to-end performance.",
        "analysis": "The official task is to find the maximum sum among all S×S windows in an N×N integer matrix (0<S<N<8000); the supplied sliding-window baseline is O(SN²). Automatic measurements favor candidate A at 0.03473 s, compared with 0.12991 s baseline. Other measured candidates are slower: B's best is 0.05732 s; C's parallel candidates are 0.05761 and 0.05829 s; a streaming ring-buffer candidate is 0.05961 s. These results support prioritizing A's flat-storage, rolling-sum serial approach while treating parallelization and reduced-memory alternatives as hypotheses to test. Preserve general correctness and 64-bit intermediate sums, and base integration decisions on measured integrated performance."
      },
